[{:db/id 17592186369017 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2k+1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k+1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2k, \\\\ k+1=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2=2k\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =2k \\\\ 1+1 & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369018 :answer-field/correct 17592186369020 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}]
